---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('klaviyo')!;
const b = appBySlug('drip')!;
const path = '/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-drip');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/klaviyo-vs-drip');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does adding to cart mean checkout has started?",
    "answer": "No. Klaviyo documents distinct triggers and recommends excluding later checkout and purchase activity from the earlier cart flow."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can I copy the event names into Drip?",
    "answer": "Map payloads, identifiers, rendering and purchase exits; similar labels do not establish equivalent behavior."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are the modeled discounts recommendations?",
    "answer": "No. The examples show gross-profit cost under fixed goods costs, with other expenses excluded."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is the migration stop condition?",
    "answer": "Pause enrollment when rendering, purchase exits or permission reconciliation fails, then reconcile pending sends before restoring the prior owner."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Compare event definitions and high-value basket economics before rebuilding recovery.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Klaviyo", "reason": "Validate the existing event and recovery workflow."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Drip", "reason": "Compare the demonstrated benefit with the complete cost of change."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Decide whether to repair or rebuild</h2>
<p>A working Klaviyo checkout flow is a strong reason to price a repair before moving to Drip. Drip is worth evaluating when a demonstrated workflow change reduces ongoing operating work enough to repay rebuilding event mappings, messages and reporting. Conversely, a Drip merchant considering Klaviyo should first identify the missing cart-stage use case. Test the specific gap instead of choosing by unsupported ratings.</p>
<h2>Klaviyo cart and checkout are separate entry points</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/35510464880027">Klaviyo’s Added to Cart guide</a> distinguishes that flow from the default Checkout Started recovery flow. Added to Cart requires the app embed, behavioral tracking and an identified shopper. Its recommended profile filters exclude shoppers who subsequently start checkout or place an order. Check both conditions in the actual flow rather than adding a second reminder stream over the existing one.</p>
<h2>Translate the event, not the display name</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702624909-Shopify-Checkout-Events">Drip documents checkout-created and checkout-updated events</a> plus item-level equivalents. Checkout creation follows an identified shopper adding items and supplying an email at checkout. Its automation email can use the checkout return URL. For migration, map the actual payload and render a sample rather than treating Klaviyo Checkout Started and every Drip item event as interchangeable.</p>
<h2>Prove the purchase exit in Klaviyo</h2>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115005080447">Klaviyo’s Shopify data reference</a> explains that Checkout Started also includes checkouts that result in purchases. Recovery therefore needs a delay and a Placed Order exclusion. It also says to disable the Shopify abandoned-checkout messages manually when replacing them. Audit the existing senders and their queues before releasing a replacement. Sources checked November 25, 2026.</p>
<h2>Proposed acceptance exercise: one shopper, two stages</h2>
<p>Start with an identified shopper adding a product without checking out, then start checkout while the cart reminder waits. Record which flow owns the next message and whether the earlier queue stops. Purchase before the next delay ends and inspect every remaining send decision. Repeat with a shopper who is not identified until checkout. A smaller earlier-stage audience can reflect tracking eligibility rather than a broken checkout integration. These are proposed acceptance checks, not a vendor benchmark.</p>
<h2>Separate basket changes from duplicate events</h2>
<p>Change the quantity, choose another size and remove one product before clicking the recovery link. Compare event timestamps, cart or checkout identifiers, template contents and the actual destination. Replay the same event, then create a second checkout with the same email. Require duplicate protection without permanently excluding a returning buyer. Check a local-currency checkout separately so a value-based branch does not compare unlike currencies.</p>
<h2>High-value baskets need an objection path</h2>
<p>For international shipping shock, show accurate delivery costs and terms before considering an incentive. A subscription toggle needs a clear purchase choice; gift-message edits need a usable editing path. A mobile shopper may need a short return link and trust information, while a repeat buyer may not need introductory proof. Test out-of-stock size handling, financing and BNPL information, bundle discounts and minimum-order rules independently. A promotion cannot repair a broken checkout or promise an unavailable variant.</p>
<h2>Model the dollars given away</h2>
<p>Hypothetical fixed-goods-cost models exclude shipping, tax, fees, returns, message expense and platform cost. A $631 basket at 50% gross margin has $315.50 goods cost and gross profit. A 16% code reduces revenue to $530.04 and gross profit to $214.54: $100.96 spent. A $792 basket at 53% margin has $372.24 goods cost and $419.76 gross profit. An 8% code leaves $728.64 revenue and $356.40 gross profit: $63.36 spent. These are examples, not recommended caps. At those modeled profits, the discounted route needs about 1.47 and 1.18 times as many orders respectively just to match full-price gross profit, before other costs.</p>
<h2>Compare the cost of equal eligibility</h2>
<p>Quote both accounts with the same usable audience, email frequency, SMS regions and seasonal peak assumptions. Include any channel access, usage charges, implementation and contract overlap. Add recurring time to maintain product templates, segments and competing-flow exclusions. Do not treat a reduction in billable contacts caused by deleting engaged shoppers as a platform efficiency gain. Calculate the monthly savings after QA and maintenance, then compare them with the one-time migration cost and a realistic payback period.</p>
<h2>Keep attribution comparable</h2>
<p>Use the same eligible shopper cohort, offer policy and order window. Assign shoppers consistently to one recovery owner and, where traffic supports it, a randomized no-reminder group. Reconcile Shopify paid orders with discounts, refunds, variable fulfillment and channel expense. Show incremental contribution and reachable audience, not only credited revenue. Do not interpret a change in attribution window or trigger coverage as proof that the replacement produces more demand.</p>
<h2>Migrate mappings and exclusions together</h2>
<p>Inventory checkout, cart and browse flows, custom event sources, product fields, forms and external senders. Preserve email and SMS permission provenance, opt-outs and suppression reasons. Keep historical orders for segmentation only after confirming they cannot trigger fresh recovery. Choose either draining old waiting queues or canceling them at a documented cutoff; release new eligible events to one owner. Test purchase and unsubscribe updates before opening enrollment.</p>
<h2>Use a reversible decision threshold</h2>
<p>Keep the current account available while validating the replacement with controlled test profiles. Record each message already sent against the shopper and checkout. If a template or purchase exit fails, pause enrollment, resolve queued sends and restore the old owner only after reconciling that ledger. Choose Klaviyo when the existing cart/checkout separation solves the requirement at the better total cost; choose Drip when the mapped workflow passes those same cases and the resulting operating savings justify the rebuild.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
